EndpointSlice does not mean a public API endpoint. It is a Kubernetes resource that records the Pod addresses behind a Service.

Your output means:

Service medical-api
        ↓
Pod IP 10.1.0.66
        ↓
Container port 8000

We normally don’t call this directly:

http://10.1.0.66:8000

because:

Pod IP is normally reachable only inside the cluster.
Pod IP changes when the Pod is recreated.
It bypasses Service load balancing.
With several Pods, you would have several changing IPs.
The Service only sends traffic to Ready endpoints.

Instead, applications inside Kubernetes use the stable Service DNS:

http://medical-api

or the full name:

http://medical-api.medical-helm.svc.cluster.local

From Windows, we use port-forward:

kubectl port-forward -n medical-helm service/medical-api 8081:80

The flow is:

Windows localhost:8081
        ↓
Kubernetes Service:80
        ↓
EndpointSlice
        ↓
Pod 10.1.0.66:8000

So EndpointSlice is Kubernetes’ routing information, while the Service is the stable address clients should use.

